Current branch scope
- This file matches the current repository tree on branch `RF`.
- It documents the live firmware pair only:
  - `ESP_RF_Octopus`
  - `Marlin/marlin-2.1.2.6`
- Older variants that existed in previous branches or layouts are intentionally not listed here.

ESP_RF_Octopus
- Role:
  - ESP32 access point
  - web UI host
  - UART bridge to Octopus / Marlin
  - direct 8-input RF/button receiver
- PlatformIO board:
  - `esp32dev`
- USB serial monitor:
  - `115200`
- Wi-Fi AP:
  - SSID: `ESP_RF_Octopus`
  - password: `octopus123`
  - IP: `192.168.4.1`
  - max clients: `8`

ESP32 <-> Octopus wiring
- UART to Octopus:
  - ESP32 `GPIO16` (RX2) <- Octopus `PD8`
  - ESP32 `GPIO17` (TX2) -> Octopus `PD9`
  - Marlin side uses `SERIAL_PORT_2 3`
  - link baud: `115200`
  - on the Octopus board this is the WIFI/UART header serial pair used by the ESP bridge
- Reset line:
  - ESP32 `GPIO23` -> Octopus `RESET`
  - output mode: `OUTPUT_OPEN_DRAIN`
  - active level: low
  - reset pulse: `250 ms`
  - common GND between ESP32 and Octopus is required
  - do not drive the Octopus reset line high from the ESP32

ESP32 RF/button inputs
- All button inputs use `INPUT_PULLUP` and are active-low.
- Mapping:
  - `GPIO13` -> `LIGHT_ON`
  - `GPIO14` -> `LIGHT_OFF`
  - `GPIO25` -> `POS1`
  - `GPIO26` -> `POS2`
  - `GPIO27` -> `RANDOM`
  - `GPIO32` -> `DIMMER`
  - `GPIO33` -> `POS3`
  - `GPIO21` -> `RESERVE`

ESP32 action mapping
- `LIGHT_ON` -> turn lamp on and start looped `M215 S1`
- `LIGHT_OFF` -> turn lamp off and stop motion with the existing stop / abort sequence (`M108`, `M215 X`, `M410`)
- `DIMMER` -> step `M355 P...` brightness while held
- `POS1` -> `M215 P1`
- `POS2` -> `M215 P2`
- `POS3` -> `M215 P3`
- `RANDOM` -> pick one configured random code and send `M215 S<code>`
  - the selected `M215 S<code>` loop is restarted forever until another command clears it
- `RESERVE` -> no action assigned yet

Lamp control
- `ESP_RF_Octopus` does not drive a local lamp GPIO.
- Lamp control is forwarded to Marlin with `M355`.
- The actual lamp power output is generated on the Octopus board.

